1
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:05,760
Our mixture is already at equilibrium.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:08,640
A keeps changing into B, and B keeps

3
00:00:08,720 --> 00:00:10,000
changing into A.

4
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:13,680
Rate means how much changes per unit time.

5
00:00:13,760 --> 00:00:16,800
At equilibrium, those opposing rates are equal, so

6
00:00:16,840 --> 00:00:18,800
the amounts stay steady.

7
00:00:18,920 --> 00:00:21,120
Now we briefly add more A.

8
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:23,680
Does B increase at that same instant?

9
00:00:23,760 --> 00:00:24,280
No.

10
00:00:24,480 --> 00:00:26,640
We added A, not B.

11
00:00:26,720 --> 00:00:28,640
Any extra B has to be made by

12
00:00:28,720 --> 00:00:29,920
reaction.

13
00:00:30,040 --> 00:00:32,480
Keep those two moments separate: what we change

14
00:00:32,680 --> 00:00:35,440
directly, and what the reaction does afterwards.

15
00:00:36,967 --> 00:00:40,246
Use our simple one-to-one model: one A can

16
00:00:40,327 --> 00:00:42,966
become one B, and one B can become

17
00:00:43,047 --> 00:00:43,686
one A.

18
00:00:44,247 --> 00:00:47,206
In this model, each direction's rate depends on

19
00:00:47,287 --> 00:00:49,926
how much of its starting form is present.

20
00:00:50,047 --> 00:00:53,126
Concentration means amount per volume.

21
00:00:53,207 --> 00:00:56,006
We keep volume and temperature fixed, briefly add

22
00:00:56,087 --> 00:00:58,566
A, and close the container again.

23
00:00:58,647 --> 00:01:01,366
A's concentration rises immediately.

24
00:01:01,447 --> 00:01:03,046
B's does not.

25
00:01:03,127 --> 00:01:06,246
Straight after mixing, more A makes the A-to-B

26
00:01:06,407 --> 00:01:07,499
rate higher.

27
00:01:07,554 --> 00:01:10,806
The B-to-A rate is initially unchanged because B

28
00:01:10,887 --> 00:01:12,246
has not changed.

29
00:01:12,327 --> 00:01:14,406
So A is being used to make B

30
00:01:14,447 --> 00:01:16,726
faster than B is being used to make

31
00:01:16,887 --> 00:01:17,766
A.

32
00:01:17,927 --> 00:01:20,006
That is a net change towards B.

33
00:01:20,727 --> 00:01:23,046
A now falls from its jump, while B

34
00:01:23,127 --> 00:01:24,246
rises.

35
00:01:24,327 --> 00:01:27,046
Their changing concentrations bring the rates back towards

36
00:01:27,127 --> 00:01:28,246
equality.

37
00:01:28,327 --> 00:01:30,886
In this model, the new equilibrium has more

38
00:01:30,967 --> 00:01:33,526
A and more B than the original mixture.

39
00:01:33,927 --> 00:01:35,926
But A is lower than it was just

40
00:01:36,087 --> 00:01:37,046
after we added it.

41
00:01:38,567 --> 00:01:40,966
Start again from the original equilibrium.

42
00:01:41,367 --> 00:01:43,926
This time, remove some B and close the

43
00:01:44,007 --> 00:01:45,206
container again.

44
00:01:45,527 --> 00:01:48,206
B drops immediately because we took it out.

45
00:01:48,407 --> 00:01:50,086
A does not jump.

46
00:01:50,327 --> 00:01:53,926
With less B available, the B-to-A process initially

47
00:01:54,007 --> 00:01:54,766
slows.

48
00:01:54,967 --> 00:01:58,166
The A-to-B process is now faster, so more

49
00:01:58,247 --> 00:02:00,126
B is being made than used.

50
00:02:00,327 --> 00:02:03,046
B starts rising from its lowered value, while

51
00:02:03,127 --> 00:02:04,166
A falls.

52
00:02:04,487 --> 00:02:06,486
We call that response a shift to the

53
00:02:06,567 --> 00:02:09,206
right, towards B in our written equation.

54
00:02:09,607 --> 00:02:11,886
But compare B with its original level.

55
00:02:12,087 --> 00:02:14,566
In this model, it finishes below that level.

56
00:02:14,887 --> 00:02:17,526
The reaction replaces some of what we removed,

57
00:02:17,607 --> 00:02:18,606
not all of it.

58
00:02:18,807 --> 00:02:21,646
A rightward shift describes the reaction after the

59
00:02:21,687 --> 00:02:22,806
disturbance.

60
00:02:22,847 --> 00:02:25,206
It does not mean the final B concentration

61
00:02:25,287 --> 00:02:27,526
is higher than every earlier value.

62
00:02:29,034 --> 00:02:31,673
Le Chatelier's principle is a way to summarise

63
00:02:31,754 --> 00:02:32,713
these responses.

64
00:02:33,274 --> 00:02:35,833
A disturbed equilibrium tends to react in a

65
00:02:35,914 --> 00:02:38,793
direction that opposes the imposed change.

66
00:02:39,434 --> 00:02:42,233
Our model gives a reason: one direction is

67
00:02:42,394 --> 00:02:44,313
temporarily faster than the other.

68
00:02:44,874 --> 00:02:47,433
The mixture is not deciding to push back,

69
00:02:47,474 --> 00:02:50,193
and it does not undo the change completely.

70
00:02:50,634 --> 00:02:53,433
We also need a new term, the equilibrium

71
00:02:53,594 --> 00:02:55,193
constant, called K.

72
00:02:55,834 --> 00:02:58,793
It is a number describing the equilibrium relationship

73
00:02:58,874 --> 00:03:01,433
between the reacting substances for the reaction as

74
00:03:01,514 --> 00:03:03,753
written, at a particular temperature.

75
00:03:04,314 --> 00:03:06,153
We will build its expression in a later

76
00:03:06,354 --> 00:03:06,633
lesson.

77
00:03:07,354 --> 00:03:10,073
Adding or removing A or B changes the

78
00:03:10,154 --> 00:03:13,673
equilibrium concentrations, but it does not change K

79
00:03:13,754 --> 00:03:15,513
while the temperature stays the same.

80
00:03:16,074 --> 00:03:19,673
Different concentrations can still satisfy the same equilibrium

81
00:03:19,754 --> 00:03:20,553
relationship.

82
00:03:22,067 --> 00:03:24,626
Now keep the material inside and change the

83
00:03:24,707 --> 00:03:26,026
temperature instead.

84
00:03:26,307 --> 00:03:29,346
Two nitrogen dioxide molecules can join to form

85
00:03:29,507 --> 00:03:31,906
one dinitrogen tetroxide molecule.

86
00:03:32,307 --> 00:03:34,626
We call joining the forward direction in the

87
00:03:34,827 --> 00:03:35,866
displayed equation.

88
00:03:36,067 --> 00:03:37,386
It releases heat.

89
00:03:37,587 --> 00:03:41,186
A heat-releasing reaction is exothermic, and its enthalpy

90
00:03:41,267 --> 00:03:43,386
change is written as delta H.

91
00:03:43,587 --> 00:03:46,626
The negative sign identifies this forward reaction as

92
00:03:46,707 --> 00:03:47,826
heat-releasing.

93
00:03:48,067 --> 00:03:50,146
The reverse direction absorbs heat.

94
00:03:50,467 --> 00:03:52,186
That makes it endothermic.

95
00:03:52,387 --> 00:03:55,186
Heating favours the heat-absorbing direction in the new

96
00:03:55,267 --> 00:03:58,306
equilibrium, so this mixture ends with more nitrogen

97
00:03:58,387 --> 00:04:01,586
dioxide and less dinitrogen tetroxide.

98
00:04:01,827 --> 00:04:04,786
Cooling favours the heat-releasing direction and gives the

99
00:04:04,867 --> 00:04:05,946
opposite change.

100
00:04:06,147 --> 00:04:09,826
Nitrogen dioxide is brown and dinitrogen tetroxide is

101
00:04:09,907 --> 00:04:12,946
colourless, so the comparison has a visible chemical

102
00:04:13,027 --> 00:04:13,426
meaning.

103
00:04:13,827 --> 00:04:16,546
Heating does not always give more products.

104
00:04:16,787 --> 00:04:19,906
First identify which direction releases heat and which

105
00:04:20,027 --> 00:04:20,786
absorbs it.

106
00:04:22,300 --> 00:04:25,180
Heating can make reactions happen more readily because

107
00:04:25,260 --> 00:04:28,380
more particles have enough energy to overcome reaction

108
00:04:28,460 --> 00:04:29,260
barriers.

109
00:04:29,580 --> 00:04:31,900
But the opposing rates need not increase by

110
00:04:31,940 --> 00:04:33,020
the same proportion.

111
00:04:33,580 --> 00:04:35,900
At the old composition, their balance can be

112
00:04:36,060 --> 00:04:39,820
lost, and one direction temporarily changes more substance

113
00:04:39,900 --> 00:04:40,460
than the other.

114
00:04:41,340 --> 00:04:43,740
Now separate the temperature change from the later

115
00:04:43,820 --> 00:04:45,220
composition change.

116
00:04:45,500 --> 00:04:48,220
In an idealised rapid temperature change at fixed

117
00:04:48,300 --> 00:04:51,260
volume, we have not added or removed nitrogen

118
00:04:51,340 --> 00:04:53,900
dioxide or dinitrogen tetroxide.

119
00:04:54,460 --> 00:04:57,420
Their concentrations therefore do not jump just because

120
00:04:57,500 --> 00:04:58,620
temperature changed.

121
00:04:59,020 --> 00:05:01,260
They change afterwards through reaction.

122
00:05:02,140 --> 00:05:04,060
K has a value for the new temperature

123
00:05:04,140 --> 00:05:06,300
straight away; it does not wait for the

124
00:05:06,340 --> 00:05:07,260
mixture to settle.

125
00:05:07,820 --> 00:05:11,340
For our written heat-releasing forward reaction, heating lowers

126
00:05:11,420 --> 00:05:13,260
K and cooling raises it.

127
00:05:13,900 --> 00:05:16,620
Hold the new temperature fixed while the concentrations

128
00:05:16,700 --> 00:05:19,020
approach the new equilibrium balance.

129
00:05:23,400 --> 00:05:25,920
One experimental boundary matters here.

130
00:05:26,200 --> 00:05:29,480
A hydrated material contains water within its structure.

131
00:05:29,960 --> 00:05:32,200
If we warm it in an open oven,

132
00:05:32,280 --> 00:05:35,080
some water may leave into the surrounding air.

133
00:05:35,400 --> 00:05:39,160
That combines two changes: heating and removal of

134
00:05:39,200 --> 00:05:40,680
a participating substance.

135
00:05:41,320 --> 00:05:43,480
The observation cannot tell us the effect of

136
00:05:43,560 --> 00:05:44,600
temperature alone.

137
00:05:45,240 --> 00:05:49,000
For a temperature-only comparison, keep the material contained

138
00:05:49,240 --> 00:05:52,120
and state which reaction direction releases heat.

139
00:05:52,760 --> 00:05:56,440
For a water-removal comparison, hold temperature fixed and

140
00:05:56,520 --> 00:05:58,280
identify the substance leaving.

141
00:05:58,840 --> 00:06:01,080
A colour change by itself does not tell

142
00:06:01,160 --> 00:06:02,840
us which controls were used.

143
00:06:04,367 --> 00:06:06,126
Try this experiment on its own.

144
00:06:06,607 --> 00:06:09,086
Our one-to-one A and B model starts at

145
00:06:09,167 --> 00:06:10,126
equilibrium.

146
00:06:10,367 --> 00:06:12,446
Temperature and volume stay fixed.

147
00:06:12,927 --> 00:06:15,406
We remove some A, then close the container

148
00:06:15,487 --> 00:06:15,886
again.

149
00:06:16,447 --> 00:06:19,006
Which concentration changes immediately?

150
00:06:19,407 --> 00:06:21,726
Use the model's rate relationship to explain the

151
00:06:21,807 --> 00:06:23,726
direction of the reaction afterwards.

152
00:06:24,207 --> 00:06:25,246
Does K change?

153
00:06:25,807 --> 00:06:27,966
Pause here to work through those steps.

154
00:06:39,967 --> 00:06:43,246
Only A drops immediately, because only A was

155
00:06:43,327 --> 00:06:44,166
removed.

156
00:06:44,447 --> 00:06:47,886
Less A initially makes the A-to-B process slower.

157
00:06:48,527 --> 00:06:51,006
B has not changed yet, so the B-to-A

158
00:06:51,087 --> 00:06:52,926
rate is initially unchanged.

159
00:06:53,567 --> 00:06:56,126
B is now being converted to A faster

160
00:06:56,207 --> 00:06:58,126
than A is being converted to B.

161
00:06:58,767 --> 00:07:01,246
A starts recovering from its drop, while B

162
00:07:01,327 --> 00:07:02,126
decreases.

163
00:07:02,687 --> 00:07:05,006
K stays the same because the temperature stayed

164
00:07:05,087 --> 00:07:05,566
fixed.

165
00:07:06,527 --> 00:07:10,126
If you made both concentrations jump immediately, separate

166
00:07:10,167 --> 00:07:12,366
the removal we imposed from the reaction that

167
00:07:12,447 --> 00:07:13,166
follows.

168
00:07:14,667 --> 00:07:16,666
Now use a separate experiment.

169
00:07:16,907 --> 00:07:19,546
A closed mixture starts at equilibrium for two

170
00:07:19,627 --> 00:07:22,906
nitrogen dioxide forming dinitrogen tetroxide.

171
00:07:23,307 --> 00:07:26,426
The displayed forward direction releases heat, so it

172
00:07:26,507 --> 00:07:27,546
is exothermic.

173
00:07:27,947 --> 00:07:29,946
We cool the mixture and keep its volume

174
00:07:30,107 --> 00:07:30,706
fixed.

175
00:07:30,907 --> 00:07:33,786
Will the new equilibrium contain more or less

176
00:07:33,867 --> 00:07:35,506
dinitrogen tetroxide?

177
00:07:35,707 --> 00:07:38,066
Will K be larger or smaller?

178
00:07:38,267 --> 00:07:41,186
Explain using the direction that releases heat.

179
00:07:41,387 --> 00:07:43,066
Pause here to give your reason.

180
00:07:55,067 --> 00:07:58,066
Cooling favours the direction that releases heat.

181
00:07:58,347 --> 00:08:01,146
Here that is the forward joining reaction, so

182
00:08:01,227 --> 00:08:04,746
the new equilibrium contains more dinitrogen tetroxide.

183
00:08:05,387 --> 00:08:08,826
For this written exothermic reaction, K is larger

184
00:08:08,907 --> 00:08:10,106
at the lower temperature.

185
00:08:10,827 --> 00:08:13,546
If you chose less product because cooling slows

186
00:08:13,627 --> 00:08:17,146
reactions, separate how quickly the mixture changes from

187
00:08:17,187 --> 00:08:19,466
the final equilibrium it approaches.

188
00:08:20,967 --> 00:08:22,966
Start with what changed directly.

189
00:08:23,047 --> 00:08:27,126
Then ask which reaction direction is initially faster

190
00:08:27,447 --> 00:08:30,046
and how that changes the mixture afterwards.

191
00:08:30,327 --> 00:08:32,566
Adding or removing a substance can change its

192
00:08:32,647 --> 00:08:34,526
concentration immediately.

193
00:08:34,807 --> 00:08:37,846
Reaction changes the mixture later, and K stays

194
00:08:37,927 --> 00:08:40,166
the same if temperature stays fixed.

195
00:08:40,407 --> 00:08:43,846
For heating or cooling, first identify the heat-releasing

196
00:08:43,927 --> 00:08:45,886
and heat-absorbing directions.

197
00:08:46,167 --> 00:08:48,166
Temperature can change K as well as the

198
00:08:48,247 --> 00:08:49,286
final composition.

199
00:08:49,687 --> 00:08:52,326
Keep the written equation and conditions clear.

200
00:08:52,887 --> 00:08:55,126
Next, we will change the volume of gaseous

201
00:08:55,207 --> 00:08:58,006
equilibria and compare that with a catalyst.
